-- js-ambient.rofl — THE AMBIENT SURFACE: WHICH FREE NAME IS WHICH SURFACE, AND
-- THE MAP INTO THE LATTICE. Both DERIVED, and that is the whole of the item.
--
-- THERE IS NO `facts/js-ambient.rofl` AND THE ABSENCE IS THE HEADLINE. The
-- effect layer's own note says the map into the lattice "is a table somebody
-- has to build member by member", and building it here by hand is exactly what
-- `f_the_effect_taxonomy_already_exists_and_it_is_a_lattice` refused: a
-- concrete effect somebody TYPED is the taxonomy being invented after all. So
-- this pack authors NOT ONE effect row. Every `ambient_effect` here comes from
-- a table another layer already wrote for another purpose and defends line by
-- line — `host_module_effect`, `host_member_effect`, `host_global_effect` in
-- facts/js-host.rofl, twenty-two modules, seven member overrides and
-- twenty-two globals — and the two halves this file adds are a JOIN and a
-- TRANSLATION, both of which can be argued with as rules.
--
-- WHAT IT PLUGS INTO. rules/js-effects.rofl declares three relations `edb` and
-- waits for them:
--
--   ambient_binding(File, Name, Surface)         which free NAME is which surface
--   surface_origin(Surface, Origin)              es_intrinsic | host_runtime |
--                                                builtin_prototype
--   ambient_effect(Surface, Member, EffectName)  the map, ONE ROW PER MEMBER
--
-- THE MEMBER IS A STRING, and the distinction is not style: the operation half
-- arrives from `selects[flow]`, which carries a key as a STRING, so
-- `ambient_effect(fs, readFileSync, io)` loads, holds, joins nothing and
-- reports nothing. Nothing in this file is written as a constant — every
-- surface and every member is a variable bound from a generated table — which
-- is the one construction in which that defect cannot occur.
--
-- WHAT IT NEEDS LOADED. `rules/js-effects.rofl` (the lattice),
-- `rules/js-host.rofl` + its two fact packs (the runtime surface),
-- `rules/js-globals.rofl` + `facts/js-globals.rofl` (the ES globals). A world
-- holding this pack and not those reports the missing premises as undefined
-- rather than deriving a smaller answer, which is the right thing for it to do.

-- THE CROSSING. This pack's contract relations are [main] — that is where
-- rules/js-effects.rofl reads them, unbracketed — and their premises are the
-- scanner's book: `free_global[code]`, `host_module_ns[code]`,
-- `member_effect[code]`. Saying so is one line, and boot.rofl's own note about
-- `imports(main, $kernel)` is why it is said rather than left to the fact that
-- a single undeclared hop is invisible to `crossing`.
imports(main, code).
imports(flow, code).
imports(flow, main).
imports(audit, code).
imports(audit, flow).
imports(audit, main).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 1. THE SURFACES AND WHERE EACH ONE'S NAME CAME FROM
--
-- `surface_origin` is the relation that keeps the concrete column DERIVED: a
-- surface that cannot say which declaration file it came from is a name
-- somebody typed, and `concrete_no_origin[audit]` refuses it. All four arms
-- below read a GENERATED table, so no surface in this model can fail to name
-- one.
--
-- THE SURFACE IS THE NAME THE DECLARATION USES AND NOT A NICKNAME. A host
-- module's surface is its canonical specifier — `"node:fs"`, the string
-- `builtin_canonical[code]` produces — and a global's surface is its own name.
-- Coining `fs` for `"node:fs"` would have been a name this pack has no
-- authority over, in a column whose entire argument is that its names come
-- from a declaration file.
surface_origin(Spec, host_runtime)   :- host_module(node, Spec).
surface_origin(Name, host_runtime)   :- host_global(_, Name).
surface_origin(Name, es_intrinsic)   :- lib_global(Name, _, _).
surface_origin(P, builtin_prototype) :- builtin_prototype(P).

-- ...AND THE ONE WAY THAT TABLE CAN BE WRONG, as a row. `scanners/host_lib.ts`
-- SUBTRACTS the ECMAScript baseline from both hosts on purpose — "crediting a
-- host with `JSON` or `Math` would make `host_global` a second, rival copy of a
-- table this repository already generates from a better source" — and this is
-- that sentence as something that can go red. A surface with two origins is
-- two different declaration files claiming one name, and every effect
-- attributed to it would be attributed twice.
surface_two_origins[audit](S, A, B) :- surface_origin(S, A), surface_origin(S, B), A != B.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 2. WHICH FREE NAME IS WHICH SURFACE
--
-- TWO DOORS AND NOT THREE, which is rules/js-host.rofl's design read from the
-- other side. A NAMESPACE stands for a whole surface and a member off it is
-- that surface's member; a FREE GLOBAL stands for itself. A NAMED IMPORT is
-- neither — `import { join } from "node:path"` binds a FUNCTION, not the module
-- — so it gets no `ambient_binding` row at all and is handled by its own site
-- rule in section 5. Emitting one would have said `join.foo()` is a member of
-- `node:path`, which is false about every named import in existence.
ambient_binding(File, Local, Spec) :- host_module_ns[code](File, Local, Spec).

-- THE GLOBAL DOOR READS `free_global[code]` AND NOT `host_global_ref[code]`,
-- and the difference is measured rather than stylistic. `host_global_ref` joins
-- `ident_in[code]` against the surface with NO reference-position restriction,
-- so every identifier of a matching name qualifies — including a PROPERTY name.
-- Over the five shared call fixtures it reports `length` and `toString` as
-- references to browser globals, because `x.length` and `y.toString` write
-- those words as identifiers. `free_global[code]` stands on
-- `global_ref_position`, which is the five positions a reference can occupy, so
-- it cannot make that mistake. Attributing io to a program that does none is
-- the failure rules/js-host.rofl's own ceiling paragraph says this direction
-- must avoid.
ambient_binding(File, Name, Name) :- free_global[code](_, Name, File), host_global(_, Name).
ambient_binding(File, Name, Name) :- free_global[code](_, Name, File), lib_global(Name, _, _).

-- a binding whose surface no declaration file claims. Empty by construction —
-- all three arms above join a surface table — and it is here because those arms
-- are the place a fourth door would be added without one.
ambient_binding_unowned[audit](File, Name, S) :- ambient_binding(File, Name, S),
                                                 not surface_origin(S, _).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 3. THE TRANSLATION — A HOST EFFECT ATOM IS A LATTICE LANDMARK, DERIVED
--
-- facts/js-host.rofl attributes an API one of eight atoms: `total`, `exn`,
-- `div`, `ndet`, `alloc`, `read`, `write`, `io`. Six of the eight are already
-- `eff_name` landmarks. TWO ARE NOT, and they are not because they are LABELS
-- rather than names: Koka parameterises `read` and `write` by a heap, and
-- `read` alone denotes nothing in this lattice.
--
-- THE HEAP IS NOT A CHOICE MADE HERE. rules/js-effects.rofl already decides it,
-- and this reads that decision rather than repeating it: `eff_heap_of[flow]`
-- says a receiver the value layer cannot trace to an allocation in this program
-- is `global`. An ambient surface is BY DEFINITION not allocated in this
-- program — that is what makes it ambient — so an ambient read is `read<global>`
-- and an ambient write is `write<global>`, with no arm and no exception.
edb(amb_heap).
amb_heap(none).
amb_heap(global).
amb_atom_label(A, H) :- host_effect_atom(A), eff_label(A, H), amb_heap(H).

-- ...AND THE LANDMARK IS THE LEAST NAME CONTAINING THAT LABEL, computed by the
-- lattice's own closure rather than by a table of eight rows. That is the same
-- Moore-family argument `effect_of[flow]` stands on, applied to a singleton row
-- instead of to a function's: it exists and is unique exactly because
-- `join_missing`, `join_ambiguous`, `meet_missing` and `meet_ambiguous` are
-- empty, which rules/js-effects.rofl asserts. A hand-written
-- `eff_of_host(read, rd_global).` would have been eight rows nobody could
-- argue with; this is a derivation that moves if the lattice does.
amb_short(A, N)      :- amb_atom_label(A, H), eff_name(N), not eff_row(N, A, H).
amb_covers(A, N)     :- amb_atom_label(A, _), eff_name(N), not amb_short(A, N).
amb_covers_low(A, N) :- amb_covers(A, M), eff_lt(M, N).
eff_of_host(A, N)    :- amb_covers(A, N), not amb_covers_low(A, N).
-- ...and an atom that is a landmark and NOT a label denotes itself. `total` and
-- `io` are the two: neither is in `eff_label`, so the arm above cannot reach
-- them and this one says the obvious thing once.
eff_of_host(A, A)    :- host_effect_atom(A), eff_name(A), not eff_label(A, _).

-- THE TWO WAYS THAT TRANSLATION CAN BE WRONG, and both are rows rather than a
-- comment. An atom with no landmark is an attribution that silently disappears;
-- an atom with two is the ambiguity the lattice audits forbid arriving through
-- a surface instead of through a pair.
host_atom_unmapped[audit](A)        :- host_effect_atom(A), not eff_of_host(A, _).
host_atom_two_names[audit](A, X, Y) :- eff_of_host(A, X), eff_of_host(A, Y), X != Y.

-- ===========================================================================
-- 4. `ambient_effect` — THE MAP, ONE ROW PER MEMBER, DERIVED
--
-- 4a. THE MODULE HALF, AND IT IS CORPUS-INDEPENDENT. `member_effect[code]` in
-- rules/js-host.rofl is the module default with the member override subtracted
-- — the pattern `path.resolve` exists to justify — and it ranges over
-- `host_module_member`, which the TypeScript checker enumerated. So this arm
-- produces a row for every member of every attributed module whether or not any
-- program in any corpus calls it, which is what a surface table should be.
ambient_effect(Spec, Key, E) :- member_effect[code](Spec, Key, A), eff_of_host(A, E).

-- 4b. THE GLOBAL HALF, AND IT IS NOT, AND THE REASON IS A GAP IN THE SCANNER
-- RATHER THAN A CHOICE HERE. `scanners/host_lib.ts` walks
-- `checker.getTypeAtLocation(init).getProperties()` for a MODULE and gets 1321
-- members; for the globals it walks the properties of `globalThis` and stops at
-- their NAMES. So `host_global(node, "console")` exists and there is no
-- `host_global_member(node, "console", "log")` anywhere in the tree — the same
-- checker call one level down would produce it, and that is a separate item
-- (`w_ambient_global_members`).
--
-- WHAT THIS PACK DOES WITH THAT, stated rather than smuggled: a global's effect
-- is its SURFACE DEFAULT, and the members it lands on are the ones a program in
-- the corpus actually selects. It is the same default-with-override shape as
-- 4a with the override table empty and the member list supplied by the corpus,
-- and `ambient_unenumerated[flow]` below is the row that says which surfaces
-- are in that state.
ambient_global_default(Name, E) :- host_global_effect(_, Name, A), eff_of_host(A, E).
amb_global_member[flow](Name, Key) :- free_global[code](O, Name, _),
                                      ast_child[code](M, object, 0, O),
                                      selects[flow](M, Key),
                                      host_global_effect(_, Name, _).
ambient_effect(Name, Key, E)       :- amb_global_member[flow](Name, Key),
                                      ambient_global_default(Name, E).

-- ...AND THE TWO OPERATIONS THAT ARE NOT MEMBERS. `fetch(u)` calls the surface
-- ITSELF and `new URL(s)` CONSTRUCTS it; neither selects a key, so neither has
-- a member name, and writing the surface's own name in the operation column
-- would make the two shapes indistinguishable in the one relation that exists
-- to tell them apart. `itself` is rules/js-host.rofl's word for the first and
-- is borrowed rather than coined; `construct` is new here, because the host
-- layer has no `new` shape at all — its own fixture records that
-- `new TextDecoder().decode(b)` could not be its site.
edb(amb_operation_word).
amb_operation_word(itself).
amb_operation_word(construct).
ambient_effect(Name, W, E) :- amb_operation_word(W), ambient_global_default(Name, E).

-- WHICH SURFACES THIS MODEL CAN LIST THE MEMBERS OF. A positive relation and
-- not an audit, for the reason CLAUDE.md gives: it is non-empty on an honest
-- tree BY DESIGN, and it is the row that says how much of the attributed
-- surface is corpus-bounded rather than declared.
ambient_enumerated(S)         :- host_module_member(node, S, _).
ambient_unenumerated[flow](S) :- ambient_effect(S, _, _), not ambient_enumerated(S).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 5. THE THREE SITE SHAPES THE EFFECT PACK'S PLUG DOES NOT COVER
--
-- `eff_surface[flow]` in rules/js-effects.rofl has two arms and both read
-- `ast_child(N, object, 0, O)` — a MEMBER call. That is the shape
-- `console.log(x)` and `fs.readFileSync(p)` have, and it is one of four ways to
-- reach an ambient callee. The other three are here, in this pack, because they
-- are what `ambient_binding` alone cannot express: two of them have no member
-- at all and the third's member is the IMPORTED name rather than the selected
-- one.

-- 5a. A PLAIN CALL OF A NAMED IMPORT — `join(a, b)`. The local name is the
-- BINDING and the imported name is the MEMBER, so `import { readFile as rf }`
-- resolves to `node:fs/promises` + `"readFile"` with no rename test, which is
-- the move `host_module_named[code]` already makes and this reads.
amb_named_call[flow](C, Spec, Key) :- unresolved_call[code](C, _), callee_of[code](C, N),
                                      ident_in[code](N, Local, File),
                                      host_module_named[code](File, Local, Spec, Key).
eff_surface[flow](C, Spec)  :- amb_named_call[flow](C, Spec, _).
eff_operation[flow](C, Key) :- amb_named_call[flow](C, _, Key).

-- 5b. A PLAIN CALL OF AN AMBIENT GLOBAL — `fetch(u)`, `structuredClone(v)`,
-- `BigInt(n)`.
amb_global_call[flow](C, Name) :- unresolved_call[code](C, _), callee_of[code](C, N),
                                  free_global[code](N, Name, File),
                                  ambient_binding(File, Name, Name).
eff_surface[flow](C, Name)     :- amb_global_call[flow](C, Name).
eff_operation[flow](C, itself) :- amb_global_call[flow](C, _).

-- 5c. A CONSTRUCTION — `new URL(s)`, `new Map()`. THIS IS THE HALF OF
-- `new_expression` THAT WAS OPEN: the allocation is seeded for all of them by
-- `eff_alloc_kind`, and what a constructor DOES beyond allocating is this row.
-- A `new_expression` is a `transfer_site` and not a `call_site`, so
-- `unresolved_call` cannot see it and the negation is written against
-- `resolved_site[code]` — the constructor edge rules/js-callgraph.rofl added
-- resolves `new Box()` to a class in this program, and one that resolves is not
-- ambient.
amb_construct[flow](X, Name) :- transfer_site[code](X, new_expression),
                                not resolved_site[code](X),
                                ast_child[code](X, callee, 0, N),
                                free_global[code](N, Name, File),
                                ambient_binding(File, Name, Name).
eff_surface[flow](X, Name)        :- amb_construct[flow](X, Name).
eff_operation[flow](X, construct) :- amb_construct[flow](X, _).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 6. `identifier` — THE THIRD CASE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF THAT CELL
--
-- rules/js-effects.rofl derives two of the three: a name something in this
-- program reassigns is `read<local>`, and a name nothing reassigns is a VALUE
-- with no cell to read, which is Koka's answer and not a shortcut. The third is
-- a FREE GLOBAL, and it is a read of a property of the host's global object —
-- a cell this program did not allocate and cannot trace, which is `global` by
-- exactly the test `eff_heap_of[flow]` applies to a receiver.
--
-- THE CEILING IS `global_ref_position` AND IT IS NOT MINE. rules/js-globals.rofl
-- names five reference positions — the object of a member expression, of an
-- optional member expression, and the callee of a call, an optional call and a
-- `new` — so a BARE mention is invisible: `n > 0 ? n : Infinity` and
-- `typeof Promise` read a global and seed nothing here. That is stated in that
-- pack and asserted by name in its test; this rule inherits it exactly.
eff_here[flow](E, read, global) :- free_global[code](E, _, _).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 7. THE FRONTIER, AS POSITIVE RELATIONS
--
-- Neither of these is an `[audit]` and both are non-empty on an honest tree,
-- which is the distinction CLAUDE.md records: a gate red on an honest checkout
-- gets switched off. rules/js-effects.rofl already has `concrete_unmapped[flow]`
-- per (surface, member); these are the coarser question the queue actually
-- asks, which is WHICH SURFACE nobody has attributed and therefore who owes it.
amb_surface_attributed(S)              :- ambient_effect(S, _, _).
ambient_surface_unattributed[flow](S)  :- concrete_effect[flow](_, S, _),
                                          not amb_surface_attributed(S).
-- ...and the same question with the ORIGIN in the row, because the answer is
-- different per origin and that is the finding this item leaves behind: the
-- host surface has an effect table, the ECMAScript one has none, and the
-- builtin prototypes have none either.
ambient_owed[flow](O, S) :- ambient_surface_unattributed[flow](S), surface_origin(S, O).

-- ===========================================================================
-- 8. THE ORACLE THIS PACK BREAKS, AND THE STRONGER ONE THAT REPLACES IT
--
-- `eff_exn_only[audit]` and `may_throw_only[audit]` in rules/js-effects.rofl
-- are the fifth layer's ONE independent oracle: the `exn` projection of the
-- general machinery must reproduce `may_throw[code]` ROW FOR ROW. That
-- agreement was true and is now false in ONE DIRECTION, and the reason is this
-- pack rather than a defect in either relation.
--
-- `may_throw[code]` is seeded by `throw_statement` AND NOTHING ELSE — its own
-- pack says so, and rules/js-effects.rofl copies the seed deliberately so that
-- the two can be compared. `io` is `<st<global>, div, exn, ndet>`, so the
-- moment an ambient call is attributed `io` the enclosing function carries
-- `exn`, and `console.log` really can throw. THE EFFECT LAYER IS NOW RIGHT AND
-- THE ORACLE IS NOW INCOMPLETE; measured over the ambient world, twelve
-- functions and every one of them reaches an ambient `io`.
--
-- SO THE ORACLE IS NARROWED RATHER THAN DROPPED, and what is left is strictly
-- stronger than a count would have been: `may_throw_only` must still be EMPTY
-- in both trees (this layer may not LOSE a throw the exception layer has), and
-- an `eff_exn_only` function that reaches no ambient `exn` source is a real
-- divergence. The carrier closure below is the propagation rule's own shape —
-- one hop per `resolves` edge, minus what a handler discharges — so a
-- divergence cannot hide behind the call graph.
amb_exn_source[flow](C)  :- concrete_effect[flow](C, S, Op), ambient_effect(S, Op, E),
                            eff_row(E, exn, none).
amb_exn_carrier[flow](F) :- amb_exn_source[flow](C), nearest_v[flow](F, C).
amb_exn_carrier[flow](F) :- amb_exn_carrier[flow](G), resolves[code](C, G),
                            nearest_v[flow](F, C), not eff_discharged_at[code](C, exn).
eff_exn_unexplained[audit](F) :- eff_exn_only[audit](F), not amb_exn_carrier[flow](F).
